  <!-- SECTION 3 -->
  <section id="north-america-rules" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="North American DST Rules">
    <h2>3. North American DST Rules &amp; Calendar Calculations</h2>
    <p>
      In the United States and Canada, Daylight Saving Time rules are codified by federal statute (the Energy Policy Act of 2005):
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Start Rule</strong>: Second Sunday in March at 02:00 AM local time (clocks jump to 03:00 AM EDT). During summer, the US East Coast operates on Eastern Daylight Time (<a href="/est-vs-edt/">EDT, UTC-4</a>), while winter reverts to Eastern Standard Time (<a href="/blog/what-is-est/">EST, UTC-5</a>).
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>End Rule</strong>: First Sunday in November at 02:00 AM local time (clocks jump back to 01:00 AM EST).
    </p>
    <p>
      For broader timezone details, see our <a href="/usa-time-zones/">USA time zones guide</a> and <a href="/canada-time-zones/">Canada time zones guide</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 4 -->
  <section id="european-rules" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="European Union Rules">
    <h2>4. European Union &amp; United Kingdom Calendar Rules</h2>
    <p>
      The European Union and the United Kingdom observe Summer Time under EU Directive 2000/84/EC:
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Start Rule</strong>: Last Sunday in March at 01:00 UTC. The UK shifts from <a href="/blog/what-is-gmt/">GMT (UTC+0)</a> to <a href="/bst-to-est/">BST (UTC+1)</a>, while Central Europe moves from <a href="/cet-to-est/">CET (UTC+1)</a> to <a href="/cest-to-est/">CEST (UTC+2)</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>End Rule</strong>: Last Sunday in October at 01:00 UTC.
    </p>
    <p>
      For specific transition dates and regional breakdowns, consult our <a href="/dst-start-end-dates/">DST start and end dates</a> page and our guide on <a href="/countries-that-use-dst/">countries that use DST</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 5 -->
  <section id="southern-hemisphere-rules" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Southern Hemisphere Rules">
    <h2>5. Southern Hemisphere Calendar Rules (Australia &amp; NZ)</h2>
    <p>
      Because summer occurs from December to February in the Southern Hemisphere, DST seasons cross calendar year boundaries:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Australia (NSW, VIC, SA, TAS, ACT)</strong>: Starts on the <em>first Sunday in October</em> and ends on the <em>first Sunday in April</em>. Review details in our <a href="/australia-time-zones/">Australia time zones guide</a> or converters such as <a href="/aest-to-est/">AEST to EST</a> and <a href="/acst-to-est/">ACST to EST</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>New Zealand</strong>: Starts on the <em>last Sunday in September</em> and ends on the <em>first Sunday in April</em>.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 6 -->
  <section id="leap-year-dynamics" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Leap Year Dynamics">
    <h2>6. Leap Year Dynamics (2028 Calendar Shift)</h2>
    <p>
      Leap years (such as 2028) introduce an extra day (February 29), shifting day-of-week alignments for March dates. However, positional algorithms ("second Sunday in March") automatically adjust, keeping transitions on Sundays.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 7 -->
  <section id="developer-rule-engine" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Developer Rule Engine">
    <h2>7. Developer Rules Engine &amp; IANA Time Zone Database</h2>
    <p>
      Software systems should rely on the IANA Time Zone Database (tzdb) rather than hardcoded dates. For details on programmatic date strings and timestamps, explore our technical guides on <a href="/iso-8601-date-time-format/">ISO 8601 syntax</a> and <a href="/unix-time-explained/">Unix time explained</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 8 -->
  <section id="travel-logistics" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Travel Logistics">
    <h2>8. International Travel &amp; Flight Scheduling Considerations</h2>
    <p>
      Travelers crossing international time zones during March and October transition windows should verify local arrival times using online converters such as <a href="/gmt-to-est/">GMT to EST</a>, <a href="/est-to-gmt/">EST to GMT</a>, and <a href="/gmt-vs-est/">GMT vs EST</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      For economic perspectives on timekeeping shifts, read our guides on the <a href="/business-impact-of-dst/">business impact of DST</a>, <a href="/why-dst-exists/">why DST exists</a>, and the <a href="/dst-history/">history of DST</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 9: FAQ SECTION -->
